From 7c8d1c580fcc7639f55e8c72674a5b712a257b4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velnox AI <noreply@codebuff.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:57:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(auth): accept native Google ID tokens from the Android
 clients
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Android apps cannot use the browser OAuth flow — a Custom Tab cannot hand
cookies back to the app and an embedded WebView is blocked by Google — so they
get a Google ID token from Credential Manager and need somewhere to exchange it.

POST /api/auth/native/google reuses verifyGoogleIdentity and resolveUser
unchanged, so identity resolution stays the single implementation: Google's
stable sub first, normalised email second, create only if neither exists. One
person still maps to one user row, and the response carries the same JWT the
browser gets in the velnox_session cookie — set as that cookie too, so per-user
rate limiting and the WebSocket handshake treat a native session identically.

verifyGoogleIdentity previously checked only the audience, which a valid
signature does not imply: it now also checks issuer, expiry explicitly rather
than inferring it from tokeninfo's status code, and an optional nonce, and
throws a coded GoogleTokenError so the route can answer 401 with a reason the
client can act on instead of a generic failure. The browser callback passes no
nonce and is otherwise unaffected.

member-login now also returns its token in the body: the web client ignores it,
but without it the Android VelCenter sign-in received a session it could not see.

No schema change — the nonce is checked against the token's own claim.
